content:
The upgrade manifest is the declaration that ties a document type's versions together. Its shape, from `packages/shared/document-model/upgrades.ts`:

```ts
export type UpgradeManifest<TVersions extends readonly number[]> = {
  documentType: string;
  latestVersion: TupleMember<TVersions>;   // e.g. 1 | 2 | 3 for [1, 2, 3]
  supportedVersions: TVersions;            // the tuple itself, [1, 2, 3]
  upgrades: {
    // keys: "v2" | "v3" | ...  (no "v1")
    [V in Exclude<TupleMember<TVersions>, 1> as `v${V}`]: UpgradeTransition;
  };
};

export type UpgradeTransition = {
  toVersion: number;
  upgradeReducer: UpgradeReducer<any, any>;
  description?: string;
};
```

The `Exclude<..., 1>` in the mapped type is the load-bearing part: **v1 is structurally unrepresentable as an upgrade key**, because there is no version to upgrade from. A manifest is written `upgrades: { v2 }`, then `{ v2, v3 }`, and TypeScript rejects a `v1` entry outright. Each transition names only its *target* (`toVersion`); the source is implied by position in the chain, so multi-step upgrades compose by walking v1→v2→v3 rather than declaring every pair.

One source behaviour worth internalising alongside this: `normalizeDocumentModelVersion` treats a document stamped `0` or nothing at all as version **1**, not as "latest". Documents predating versioning are legacy v1 documents and get the v1 reducer. Resolving them to latest instead would re-pin an old document's whole history onto whichever module happens to be newest — the exact corruption versioning exists to prevent.

The generated `upgrades/` folder mirrors this: `versions.ts` exports the tuple and `latestVersion`, one file per transition (`v2.ts`), and `upgrade-manifest.ts` assembles them.

_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/03-Build/02-DocumentModelCreation/08-DocumentModelVersioning.md, confirmed against packages/shared/document-model/upgrades.ts (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._
